From: claude <claude@p7x.dev>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 21:13:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mail: Add just committed to report patches trunk took
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Change-Id: spvykvtxplmuntnoppyqtzuxrluqyyyw

Patches are applied from the list without a reply, so nothing on the
list says which ones landed, and the review queue cannot be cleared
without checking trunk by hand.

The change id cannot link a patch to its commit, because git am drops
it.  The diff survives: git patch-id --stable gives a patch and the
commit it became the same id after git am, a rebase, or a reworded
message.  Hashing every commit in ::trunk makes the check a query on
trunk's whole history rather than its last few commits, and it takes
a fraction of a second on today's 1200 commits.  The answer depends
only on trunk and the list, so every run gives the same one.

Each match gets a reply carrying a Committed header that names the
patch's Message-ID.  Scripts can tag from that header without
parsing prose, and a patch already named in one is skipped, so a
second run is a no-op.  A patch edited before it was applied has a
new patch-id and is not found.
---
This applies to the mail branch; base-commit is mail@upstream.  It
supersedes "[PATCH] mail: Add applied.sh to find patches trunk
applied" [1], which searched only a given range of trunk and printed
its matches instead of reporting them.  "[PATCH] notmuch: Tag patches
reported committed" [2], on trunk, turns the Committed header into a
committed tag.

Patch-id rather than applying each open patch to trunk and checking
for an empty result: plain git am fails on an applied patch rather
than coming out empty, and git am -3 reports "already applied" only
while no later commit has touched the same lines.  Patch-id compares
each patch with the commit that took it, whatever came after.

Tested on a scratch copy of the list with its own notmuch database,
with trunk@upstream as the revision.  It delivered four replies, one
for each patch trunk took unchanged, threaded to the patch and
addressed to everyone on it.  A second run delivered none.  A patch
trunk took with edits, "[PATCH 1/2] claude: Reply to all in
process-email", was not reported.

just committed mail checks the mail branch the same way.  It finds
nothing today: the one patch the mail branch took, "[PATCH v2] mail:
Add just commit-mail", landed with edits.

